Falsifies `inter_process_communication::verification::removing_a_waiter_preserves_the_invariant`.

The defect: `remove_sender` takes the victim off and then puts it back. The function still returns
the right answer to every caller that only asks whether the waiter was there.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert!(found.is_none() || e.is_idle())`, on a hit. The invariant
assertion survives, because one queue is still empty, which is why this harness states both:
a remove that reports success while leaving the thread queued is how milestone 133 (ending a
permanently blocked thread) ends a thread and then finds it again on a wait queue.

The defect mints a second token with `unsafe`, because one token cannot be both pushed and
returned. That this takes an `unsafe` block a reviewer has to sign is the token doing its job; the
harness is still shown to catch the result.

diff --git a/crates/inter_process_communication/src/rendezvous.rs b/crates/inter_process_communication/src/rendezvous.rs
--- a/crates/inter_process_communication/src/rendezvous.rs
+++ b/crates/inter_process_communication/src/rendezvous.rs
@@ -267,7 +267,12 @@
     ///
     /// Name: ratified 2026-11-21 (UTC).
     pub fn remove_sender(&mut self, victim: NonNull<T>) -> Option<Unqueued<T>> {
-        self.senders.remove(victim)
+        let found = self.senders.remove(victim);
+        if let Some(node) = &found {
+            // SAFETY: none. This is the defect: the victim goes back on the queue it was taken off.
+            self.senders.push_back(unsafe { Unqueued::new(node.as_non_null()) });
+        }
+        found
     }
 
